# DTF tier manifest: webhook-response-body-bound
#   (artifact-keeper#6403, PR #6496)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single + secrets.webhook-key + webhooks.v2-producer
# + webhooks.mock-blackhole (`hook-sink` at 172.16.<slot>.79; its live
# listener on :80 answers POST /body/<mode>/<n> with <n> bytes of a repeating
# ASCII pattern framed by Content-Length (cl), by connection close (close) or
# as chunks (chunked), and reports how many bytes it managed to write).
#
# THE BUG
# #6403 Every webhook send (the delivery worker, a manual redelivery and the
#       test endpoint) read the receiver's whole response body into memory
#       with `response.text()` and stored it in webhook_deliveries
#       .response_body. The 30 s client timeout bounds time, not size: a fast
#       receiver answering with a large page costs backend memory on every
#       attempt and grows the delivery row by the full body.
#
# Oracle (oracle.sh): seven artifact_uploaded webhooks on one generic Local
# repository, one upload, then the stored deliveries:
#   (FIX)     the three 64 MiB answers (cl, close, chunked) are stored as the
#             first 4096 bytes of the body plus the marker
#             ` ... [truncated, 4096 of 67108864 bytes]` (cl) or
#             ` ... [truncated, 4096+ bytes]` (close, chunked), so at most
#             4 KiB plus the marker; the deliveries still succeed on the 200;
#             the receiver could not write the full 64 MiB (the backend
#             stopped reading); the backend's peak RSS (VmHWM) grows by less
#             than 32 MiB over the three deliveries. The test endpoint and a
#             manual redelivery return and store the same bounded excerpt.
#             RED on main: each row holds the whole 64 MiB, the receiver wrote
#             every byte, and the peak RSS grows by well over 100 MiB.
#   (CONTROL) both images: a small JSON answer and an answer of exactly
#             4096 bytes (cl and chunked) are stored verbatim with no marker.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: back-to-back admin calls.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem secrets.webhook-key webhooks.v2-producer webhooks.mock-blackhole"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
COVERS="6403"
# A fresh 32-byte key per run (no key material in the repository).
DTF_WEBHOOK_SECRET_KEY="$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
export DTF_WEBHOOK_SECRET_KEY
